        <div id="how-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Seal Natural Stone: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Sealing natural stone is well within reach for any DIYer, but a few steps make the difference between protection that lasts and a blotchy, uneven job. Here's the process that gives professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Clean the stone with a pH-neutral stone cleaner to lift dirt, grease, and old residue — never use acidic cleaners like vinegar on calcareous limestone or travertine. Remove any existing stains before you seal, because sealing over a stain locks it in. The surface must be spotless: sealer bonds to clean stone, not to a film of grime.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Let It Dry Completely</h3>
          <p>
            Stone must be <strong>bone dry</strong> before sealing — ideally 24-48 hours after cleaning. A penetrating sealer can't soak into pores that are already full of water, so sealing damp stone gives weak, patchy protection. Make sure indoor humidity is reasonable and that outdoor stone has had dry weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Test Absorbency with a Water Drop</h3>
          <p>
            Drip a few drops of water on the stone. If it soaks in and darkens the surface within a couple of minutes, the stone is porous and ready to seal. If the water beads and sits on top, the stone is dense or already sealed and may need little or no product. This simple test tells you how thirsty the stone is and how many coats to plan.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Apply, Then Wipe the Excess</h3>
          <p>
            Apply the sealer evenly with a brush, roller, or sprayer, working in manageable sections and keeping the surface wet with product so it can soak in. Let it dwell for the time on the label (usually 5-15 minutes), then — critically for penetrating sealers — <strong>wipe off all excess</strong> with a clean cloth before it dries. Sealer left to dry on the surface leaves a hazy residue that's hard to remove.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Apply a Second Coat, Then Cure</h3>
          <p>
            On porous stone, apply a second coat after the first has had its dwell time, again wiping off the excess. Then keep the surface dry and traffic-free for the full cure time on the label — typically 24-72 hours. The sealer needs that time to fully bond and reach its rated stain resistance, so don't rush it back into service.
          </p>
        </div>

