          <p>
            Day to day, clean sealed stone with a <strong>pH-neutral stone cleaner</strong> and avoid acidic products, which can break down the sealer and etch calcareous stone underneath. Wipe up spills promptly even on a stain proof sealer — repellency buys you time, it isn't permission to leave wine sitting overnight.
          </p>
          <p>
            To know when it's time to reseal, run the same water-drop test you used before applying: drip water on the surface, and if it soaks in and darkens the stone within a few minutes, the sealer has worn through and it's time for a fresh coat. Penetrating sealers on floors and counters typically last <strong>1-3 years</strong>, outdoor stone often less, and premium stain-proof products can go <strong>10-15 years</strong>. Resealing on schedule is what keeps natural stone looking new for decades.
          </p>
