        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            Common Stone Sealing Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>
          <p>
            A good sealer can only do its job when it's applied correctly, and the most disappointing results we see almost never come from the product itself. They come from rushed prep and a handful of avoidable errors. After a year of testing and reviewing hundreds of homeowner complaints, these are the mistakes that ruin an otherwise sound sealing job on natural stone.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Before the Stone Is Fully Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating impregnator works by soaking into the pores, and it physically cannot do that if those pores are already full of water. Sealing damp stone — whether it's residual cleaning water, fresh grout moisture, or a patio that hasn't had a stretch of dry weather — gives you weak, patchy repellency and can trap that water inside, where on calcareous limestone or travertine it leads to clouding and even spalling. Give the stone a full 24 to 48 hours to dry after cleaning, longer in humid or cool conditions, and never seal stone you can still feel cool dampness on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Excess Sealer Dry into a Hazy Film</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the single most common error with penetrating and topical sealers alike. After the dwell time, any product still sitting on the surface must be wiped off with a clean cloth before it dries. Leave it, and the carrier evaporates and leaves a cloudy, streaky haze or a sticky residue bonded to the surface — far harder to remove than it was to prevent. Work in small sections so no area dries before you get back to it, and buff off every trace of excess. The stone should look natural and dry, never glossy or smeared, once you're done.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Acid Cleaner on Calcareous Stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Limestone, travertine, and marble are calcareous, which means acid dissolves them. Reaching for vinegar, lemon, or a generic tile cleaner with citric or phosphoric acid to prep these stones will etch the surface, leaving dull, rough spots that no sealer can hide — and the etching keeps happening underneath if you continue to clean with acid after sealing. Always use a pH-neutral stone cleaner on calcareous stone, both before sealing and for ongoing maintenance. Acidic cleaners are only ever appropriate on acid-resistant siliceous stone like granite or slate, and even then with caution.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Absorbency Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Not every surface needs the same amount of product, and some dense or previously sealed stone barely needs any at all. Skipping the simple water-drop test means you're guessing — and over-applying sealer to dense stone is exactly how you end up with that hazy surface film, while under-applying on thirsty stone leaves it unprotected. Drip a little water on the stone first: if it darkens and soaks in within a couple of minutes the stone is porous and ready for sealer, and if it beads on top the stone is dense and needs little or none. Test a few spots, since porosity often varies across a single floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying an Enhancer over Wet or Cold Stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet-look enhancing sealers are unforgiving of moisture and temperature. Apply one over stone that's still damp, or in cold conditions where the product can't cure evenly, and the color enrichment comes out blotchy — dark in some areas, washed out in others — and because an enhancer's color change is permanent, that patchiness is there to stay. Enhancers should go onto bone-dry stone in mild, dry conditions, and you should always test on a hidden area or spare tile first to confirm the color and evenness before committing the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Wrong Type of Sealer for the Stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Matching the sealer to the stone matters as much as the application. Putting a film-forming topical coating on breathable calcareous stone traps moisture and invites spalling; using a thin water-based interior sealer on a porous outdoor patio leaves it under-protected against weather; and reaching for a plain impregnator when you actually wanted the rich, wet look means a disappointing, no-change finish. Decide up front whether you want a natural penetrating sealer, a stronger stain-proof impregnator, or a color-deepening enhancer, then match the carrier — low-odor water-based indoors, deeper-driving solvent-based for porous outdoor stone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION: How to Choose -->
        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            How to Choose the Right Stone Sealer
          </h2>
          <p>
            With dozens of products on the shelf, picking the right stone sealer comes down to a few clear questions about the look you want, the stone you have, and where it lives. Work through them in order and the choice usually makes itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating vs. Enhancing: Decide the Look First</h3>
          <p>
            The most important decision is whether you want the stone to keep its current appearance or take on a richer, wetter tone. A penetrating impregnator soaks below the surface and repels water and oil while leaving the stone's natural, matte look completely unchanged — the right call if you chose your stone for how it already looks. An enhancing or wet-look sealer instead deepens and saturates the color into a darker, low-sheen finish that mimics how stone appears in the rain. That choice is permanent, so settle it before anything else, and if you simply want the strongest protection with no color change, step up to a stain-proof impregnator like Dry-Treat for the most aggressive repellency we tested.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Stone Type and Porosity</h3>
          <p>
            Acid-sensitive calcareous stone — limestone, travertine, and marble — needs a breathable penetrating impregnator that lets trapped moisture escape, never a film-forming coating. Porous stone drinks in product and often wants two coats, while dense stone like granite needs far less. Use the water-drop absorbency test to gauge how thirsty your surface is, and let that tell you both the sealer type and how much to buy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Consider Indoor vs. Outdoor and Food Safety</h3>
          <p>
            Where the stone lives narrows the field further. Interior tile and counters are best served by a low-odor, water-based sealer that's comfortable to use in poorly ventilated rooms, whereas porous outdoor paving stone benefits from a deeper-driving solvent-based impregnator that can stand up to weather and UV. For kitchen countertops and any surface that touches food, confirm the product is certified food-safe once cured, as not every sealer is rated for food contact.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Prioritize Breathability and Longevity</h3>
          <p>
            On natural stone, breathability is not optional — a sealer that lets vapor pass through protects the stone from the moisture and salt damage that a trapping film would cause. Beyond that, weigh longevity against cost: budget impregnators may need resealing every year or two, while a premium stain-proof product can hold for a decade or more, which often makes it the cheaper choice over the life of the stone despite the higher upfront price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION: How Often to Reseal -->
        <div id="how-often-reseal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            How Often to Reseal Natural Stone
          </h2>
          <p>
            There's no single calendar answer to how often natural stone needs resealing, because the right interval depends on the sealer, the traffic, and the exposure. The reliable way to know is to let the stone tell you with the same water-drop test you used before the first application: drip a few drops of water on the surface and watch. If it beads up and sits on top, the sealer is still working and you can leave it alone. If it soaks in and darkens the stone within a few minutes, the sealer has worn through and it's time for a fresh coat.
          </p>
          <p>
            Traffic and use drive the timeline more than anything. A busy kitchen counter, an entry floor, or a high-traffic hallway sees constant cleaning and abrasion that wears the sealer faster, so those surfaces often need resealing every year or two. A low-traffic bathroom floor, a decorative wall, or a guest-room tile can go much longer between coats. As a rough guide, ordinary penetrating sealers last one to three years on interior floors and counters, while premium stain-proof impregnators can protect for ten to fifteen years.
          </p>
          <p>
            Outdoor stone almost always needs more frequent attention than the same product would indoors. Sun, rain, freeze-thaw cycles, and de-icing salts all break a sealer down faster, so patios, walkways, and pool surrounds typically want resealing on a tighter schedule than interior stone. Check exposed outdoor surfaces with the water-drop test at least once a year, ideally heading into the wet or freezing season, and reseal promptly once water stops beading. Staying ahead of the wear is what keeps natural stone looking new for decades.
          </p>
        </div>

